#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# directed_infinity

#% text
# `directed_infinity(z)` is the point at infinity in the direction of the complex number `z`: the product `z*inf`, which the arithmetic keeps as one coefficient per direction.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A directed infinity is the product `c*inf` of a nonzero number `c` and `inf`; `directed_infinity(1)` is `inf`, `directed_infinity(-1)` is `-inf` and `directed_infinity(im)` is `im*inf`. Every directed infinity prints as such a product, which reads back as itself.

#% item
# The coefficient is reduced to its direction: a real one to its sign, an exact complex one to the Gaussian integer `a + b*im` with coprime `a` and `b` on its ray, an inexact one to `c/abs(c)`.

#% item
# `directed_infinity()` and `directed_infinity(0)` are `complex_infinity`; `directed_infinity(z)` stays unevaluated unless `z` is a number known to be nonzero.

#% item
# Directed infinities of one direction absorb finite numbers in sums; two different directions give `failure(indeterminate, ...)`. Products multiply the directions, and a negative power is 0.

#% item
# `limit` gives a directed infinity for functions of a real variable whose real and imaginary parts have limits, at least one of them infinite.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
directed_infinity(im)
#% output
#= im*inf

#% cell
[directed_infinity(1), directed_infinity(-5), directed_infinity(2 + 2*im)]
#% output
#= [inf, -inf, (1 + im)*inf]

#% cell
[directed_infinity(), directed_infinity(0)]
#% output
#= [complex_infinity, complex_infinity]

#% cell
directed_infinity(0.6 + 0.8*im)
#% output
#= (0.6 + 0.8im)*inf

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
3*im*directed_infinity(1 + im)
#% output
#= (-1 + im)*inf

#% cell
[1 + im*inf, (im*inf)^2, 1/(im*inf)]
#% output
#= [im*inf, -inf, 0]

#% cell
inf + im*inf
#% output
#! failure(indeterminate, inf + im*inf)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% cell
limit(x*im, x -> inf)
#% output
#= im*inf

#% cell
limit(exp(im*pi/4)*x, x -> inf)
#% output
#= (1 + im)*inf

#% cell
limit(x - im*x^3, x -> -inf)
#% output
#= im*inf

